cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Nikash — an independent, cross-benchmark test bench for prompt-injection defences in AI security assistants"
message: "If you use this software in academic or industrial work, please cite it using the metadata below. A paper is in preparation."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-SURNAME"
    given-names: "REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-FIRST-NAME"
abstract: >
  Security teams hand raw logs to language models to triage alerts and
  summarise incidents, and many log fields are written by the attacker.
  Nikash scores published prompt-injection defence types on each other's
  benchmarks and always reports what each defence costs on clean logs.
  Version 1 covers alert triage with five defences on two benchmarks
  (LogInject-1.0 web and SSH logs, and CAM-LDS Linux attack chains with
  re-implemented injection) and three open-weight models. It includes a
  common log schema that marks
  attacker-controlled fields, attack generators with clean twins (direct
  instructions, context stitching and instruction-free cover stories),
  defences behind one plug-in protocol (nikash/0, JSON Lines over stdin
  and stdout), twin-conditioned attack success with Wilson intervals,
  Kendall's tau for ranking transfer, and a static public scoreboard.
  Models are open-weight and self-hosted; no third-party service is ever
  attacked.
repository-code: "https://github.com/YOUR-USER/nikash"
url: "https://YOUR-USER.github.io/nikash/"
keywords:
